<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Cottius</h1><p class="st-meta">58 BCE – 63 CE (approx.) · capital Segusio</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Alpine client kingdom around Susa whose ruler Cottius allied with Augustus and gave his name to the Cottian Alps.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In the 1st century BCE the Segusini and neighbouring Alpine peoples around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montgenevre-pass">Mont Genèvre</a> pass were ruled by a king named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/donnus">Donnus</a>, who appears in Caesar's time. His son, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-julius-cottius">Marcus Julius Cottius</a>, chose to make peace with Augustus rather than resist the Roman conquest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-julius-cottius">Cottius</a> governed fourteen Alpine communities as a Roman prefect while keeping royal status locally. He built and maintained the road over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montgenevre-pass">Mont Genèvre</a> pass, a key route between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>. In 9–8 BCE he erected the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arch-of-augustus-susa">Arch of Augustus</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susa-piedmont">Segusio</a> (Susa), whose inscription lists his peoples. The kingdom combined Celtic traditions with Roman administration.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a> restored the royal title to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-julius-cottius">Cottius's</a> son, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-julius-cottius-ii">Marcus Julius Cottius II</a>, in 44 CE. When he died without an heir around 63 CE, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nero">Nero</a> annexed the kingdom as the province of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cottian-alps">Alpes Cottiae</a>. The name survives in the Cottian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 58 BCE – 63 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Italy</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:58.55%;width:2.41%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>58 BCE – 63 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Segusio</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Donnus, Marcus Julius Cottius, Marcus Julius Cottius II</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Celtic and Roman religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Gaulish, Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>, <a href="/countries/france">France</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Cottian Alps kingdom, Kingdom of Donnus</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=2&amp;focus=kingdom-of-cottius" aria-label="Kingdom of Cottius on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4848 584 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Cottius lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5168 840L5304 942"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5168 840h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5168 840h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Segusio. <a href="/atlas?year=2&amp;focus=kingdom-of-cottius">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
